A||||4||William||Tech advice needed  from the speaker experts||||1||
Z||000000||William||02-02-2001||09:46 AM||||Hey guy's, I was caught off guard by the early arrival of my leathers seats, and need to get some 3.5 speakers immediately! Do these small speakers usually come with caps for the main driver or just the tweeters? If not, do I need to add these to protect the speakers? Do I get the caps from a best buy kind of place or radio shack perhaps? What kind do I need (I'm not running any subs!)? Also, If I add a cap to the main speaker, won't the signal then go through 2 caps on it's way to the tweeter? What problem would that cause? Thanks!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Philphine||02-02-2001||10:19 AM||||i've never seen them with main speaker caps but that dosen't mean no one makes them. radio shack should have them if you don't have a local stereo shop to go to. <P>i'd cross them over fairly high, at least 150hz. no use trying to get bass out of them. i also would get full range 3.5's with no tweeter to try to keep the highs up front as much as possible.<P> if you get them with tweeters i think the only problem with the tweeters having 2 caps to go through could be phase inversion? somebdy who has electronics fresher in their head will have to verify that. if so you would just reverse polarity of the speaker leads. that's if you can even tell a difference.  <P>ok. think i'm getting in too deep. somebody make sure i don't send him down a bad road.<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 02-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||02-02-2001||10:58 AM||||Radio shack sells what is called a "Bass Blocker" you can get it for many cut-off freqs. I would not get anything lower than 125 hz. I think Best Buy sells them too. At best buy you can get a decent pair of 3 1/2" speakers. I recommend the Polk Audio's but everyone has their own preferences. Adding the Bass Blocker won't invert phase or affect the cap for the tweeter. You will need 4 bass blockers (1 for each speaker)||||reg||1
Z||000003||William||02-02-2001||09:46 PM||||Well, I picked up those bass blockers and 2 sets of 3.5" speakers. There was not much in stock around town and my choice came down to pioneer or MB Quart 3.5's. The MB Quarts were double the price of the pioneers. They also had a titanium tweeters, which I thought might sound to harsh being 1 inch from my ears. I ended up getting the Pioneers and saving a hundred dollars. When I got home I was surprized to see that although the speakers have a peak rating of 60 watts, the recommended wattage is only 2- 15 watts. That sure don't seem like much. Did I screw up? They are going to run off the 1 1/2 DIN pioneer head unit with the 45 watt peak per channel mosfet internal amp. All the other 3.5's that have a simular peak rating have a recommended range of 5 - 30 watts? Does Pioneer rate there speakers more conservativly? Should these go back tomorrow? It's always something...........||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||02-02-2001||11:31 PM||||The ratings are for a few things. First, the rating is for full range, you will be using bass blockers (which will also cut down power going to the speaker). Second you will most likely have the fader set to the dash speakers and using the seat speakers for fill-in. Third, the stereo rating is 45W MAX and I doubt you'll have it cranked to max. Usually to get the 45W output the distortion level out of the radio's amp will sound like S**t. Finally you are dividing the power to the speakers in half because each seat has a left and a right. You should be fine. Hook them up and go.<P>BTW ~ Pioneers are probably the better choice for your application anyways. Pioneer speakers tend to be good at mid-range and OK for bass and highs.||||reg||1
